# Choosing Shell Style

Filename chooses the dialect. The shebang must match the filename.

- `*.bash` uses `#!/usr/bin/env bash` and bash-style. The file runs in bash.
- `*.sh` uses `#!/bin/sh` and posix-style. `sh` is not a specific shell, so the script must be POSIX.
- Makefile recipes and git aliases have no filename. They are POSIX. Never use bashisms there.

Git subcommands are `*.bash`.

Do not give a bash script a `.sh` extension. If an existing `.sh` file shebangs bash, it is misnamed: rename it to `.bash`. Do not rewrite it to POSIX to match the old name.
